WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.636
To trace a path back

2
00:00:01.636 --> 00:00:03.290
you need the whole table

3
00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.580
or at least all of Myers's columns.

4
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.137
Edlib traces back directly only when its estimate

5
00:00:09.203 --> 00:00:11.690
of that storage is below one mebibyte.

6
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.200
Our thousand-base pairs fit.

7
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.135
The task's pairs need thirty mebibytes

8
00:00:17.135 --> 00:00:19.190
and about two hundred seventy.

9
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.762
Yet the human reference in the task

10
00:00:21.826 --> 00:00:23.540
uses only eighty kibibytes.

11
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.749
Hirschberg found the way out

12
00:00:25.807 --> 00:00:27.262
in nineteen seventy-five

13
00:00:27.262 --> 00:00:28.600
for a related problem.

14
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.190
Cut the query at its middle row.

15
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.324
Compute the top half's last

16
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.580
row going forward.

17
00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.678
Then compute the bottom half's

18
00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:37.959
costs going backward

19
00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:39.590
by running the same code on

20
00:00:39.648 --> 00:00:40.930
both strings reversed.

21
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.132
At each column

22
00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:44.574
the two numbers add up to the best cost of

23
00:00:44.631 --> 00:00:46.960
a path that crosses the middle row there.

24
00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.681
Here the sums are five, three, one

25
00:00:50.681 --> 00:00:52.030
two and four.

26
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.236
The smallest, one, is the distance

27
00:00:55.236 --> 00:00:56.460
at column two.

28
00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.450
That is where an optimal path crosses.

29
00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.379
Pause and predict

30
00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:04.750
why is the smallest sum always the distance?

31
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.110
Every path from the top-left to the bottom-right

32
00:01:11.168 --> 00:01:13.100
crosses the middle row somewhere.

33
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.632
The sum at a column is the cheapest

34
00:01:15.695 --> 00:01:17.690
path forced through that column.

35
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.680
So the cheapest of them is

36
00:01:19.743 --> 00:01:21.320
the cheapest path of all.

37
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.947
Once the crossing is known

38
00:01:24.005 --> 00:01:26.068
the top-left and bottom-right pieces

39
00:01:26.125 --> 00:01:28.131
are two smaller alignment problems

40
00:01:28.131 --> 00:01:30.480
and the other two pieces can be dropped.

41
00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.537
Solve each the same way

42
00:01:32.608 --> 00:01:34.314
and stop when a piece is

43
00:01:34.386 --> 00:01:35.950
one row or one column.

44
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.851
Only two rows of the table are ever kept

45
00:01:38.851 --> 00:01:41.340
plus the recursion and the path itself.

46
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.560
The price of linear memory is time.

47
00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.580
The first split computes

48
00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:48.110
the whole table once.

49
00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.856
The pieces below add half as much

50
00:01:50.856 --> 00:01:52.759
then a quarter, and so on

51
00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:54.380
about twice the table.

52
00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.723
On our ninety-nine percent pair

53
00:01:56.785 --> 00:01:58.592
that is two million cells for

54
00:01:58.654 --> 00:02:00.086
a table of one million

55
00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.640
and the path it returns costs seventeen

56
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.010
exactly the distance.

57
00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.059
Edlib splits the target in half instead

58
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.157
because Myers's algorithm gives a

59
00:02:09.219 --> 00:02:11.070
whole column of costs at once.

60
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.137
It bands every half by the known distance

61
00:02:14.202 --> 00:02:16.790
and traces back directly any piece whose

62
00:02:16.855 --> 00:02:18.860
estimate is under one mebibyte.

63
00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.112
But in this task the reference

64
00:02:21.175 --> 00:02:22.824
peaks at eighty kibibytes

65
00:02:22.887 --> 00:02:24.472
so a solution must choose

66
00:02:24.535 --> 00:02:25.930
a much smaller budget.

67
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.852
So: a forward pass and a backward

68
00:02:28.923 --> 00:02:30.910
pass meet in the middle row.

69
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.220
Recurse on two pieces and drop the rest.

70
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.124
Two rows of memory cost

71
00:02:36.191 --> 00:02:37.610
about twice the time.

72
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:39.648
Next, the task itself

73
00:02:39.648 --> 00:02:41.720
and what the agents wrote.
